Q: Possible Delphi Win64 Debugger Bug: Volatile registers (RCX/RDX) truncated to 32-bit after breakpoints under heavy multithreaded load A: <p>Yes, I've just found further proof of a deeply obscure architectural flaw in the Delphi IDE ecosystem, often referred to as the <strong>64-bit Debugger Context Corruption</strong> or <strong>Register Truncation Bug</strong>. So, I'm not crazy; my code's logic is perfectly fine.</p>
<p>I managed to completely isolate this behavior from my RIP engine into a Minimal Reproducible Example (MRE) that forces the thread scheduler and the debugger into the exact race condition required to trigger the bug.</p>
<p><strong>The Proof (Minimal Reproducible Example):</strong></p>
<p>Create a new Delphi Win64 Console Application and use the following code.</p>
<p><em>DelphiDeBug.dpr:</em></p>
<pre><code>program DelphiDeBug;

{$APPTYPE CONSOLE}
{$R *.res}

uses
  System.SysUtils,
  UThreadDebug in 'UThreadDebug.pas';

const
  THREAD_COUNT = 8;

var
  i: Integer;
  th: array [0 .. THREAD_COUNT - 1] of TWorkerThread;

begin
  for i := 0 to THREAD_COUNT - 1 do
  begin
    th[i] := TWorkerThread.Create(True);
    th[i].FreeOnTerminate := False; 
    th[i].Start;
  end;

  for i := 0 to THREAD_COUNT - 1 do
    th[i].WaitFor;
end.
</code></pre>
<p><em>UThreadDebug.pas:</em></p>
<pre><code>unit UThreadDebug;

interface

uses
  System.SysUtils,
  System.Classes;

type
  TObj = class
    tag: UInt64;
    procedure Foo;
    procedure Bar;
  end;

  TWorkerThread = class(TThread)
  protected
    procedure Execute; override;
  end;

implementation

procedure TObj.Foo;
begin
  Bar;  // BREAKPOINT #1
end;

procedure TObj.Bar;
asm
  nop  // BREAKPOINT #2
  mov [rcx + TObj.tag].qword, $12345678 // The execution block. 
  // rcx will get decapitated after a few dozen of iterations
end;

procedure TWorkerThread.Execute;
var
  i: Integer;
  o: TObj;
begin
  o := TObj.Create;
  for i := 1 to 2000000 do
    o.Foo;
  o.Free;
end;

end.
</code></pre>
<p><strong>How to see the bug in action:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li><p>Set a breakpoint on <code>Bar; // BREAKPOINT #1</code> and the <code>nop // BREAKPOINT #2</code> instruction.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Run the application in Debug mode (<code>F9</code>).</p>
</li>
<li><p>Once the debugger pauses, press and hold <code>F9</code> (or mash it repeatedly) to force the threads to resume under heavy contention.</p>
</li>
<li><p>After a few dozen or so breaks, you will get an <strong>Access Violation</strong> on the <code>mov</code> instruction.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>A possible mechanism behind the buggy behavior:</strong></p>
<p>The Delphi IDE (<code>bds.exe</code>) is fundamentally a 32-bit application. To debug a 64-bit process, it uses a remote 64-bit debugging stub.</p>
<p>When a thread hits a breakpoint, the IDE pauses the thread and retrieves the processor state via the Windows API <code>GetThreadContext</code>. While paused, the IDE evaluates variables for the UI. This is probably the point where registers get decapitated for an unknown reason.</p>
<p>So, under heavy multithreaded stress, when you press <code>F9</code> to resume execution, the IDE attempts to write the context back via <code>SetThreadContext</code>. The volatile parameter registers (like <code>RCX</code>, <code>RDX</code>, <code>R8</code>, <code>R9</code>) are pushed back into the CPU, but with their upper 32 bits already zeroed out.</p>
<p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> Do not trust the Delphi IDE debugger when stepping through highly optimized, timing-sensitive 64-bit code under load. The Access Violation is an artifact of the debugger itself.</p>
